WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.610
This is where you accepted the Nobel?

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.530
Yes.
Like right here. Right

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:08.530
Here.

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:09.363
Tell me about that moment.

00:00:09.363 --> 00:00:11.400
Well, it was in the heart of the pandemic,

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.310
and normally it's done
in Stockholm, Sweden,

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:17.100
and so the Nobel Committee
said, well, we're going

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.250
to send the Nobel to you,

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.980
and we're going to give
it to you in your garden.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:26.640
When you wake up the next day,

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.070
does the world just open up,

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.250
or do you feel a weight of responsibility?

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:35.430
If I'm honest, I mean,
it was so overwhelming.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:36.960
I didn't go into science

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.940
with any thoughts of winning prizes.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.230
Believe me. You know, I just hoped I

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:41.790
could get a job someday.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:43.020
My husband said to me, well,

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.200
now your main job is being
an ambassador for science.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.610
And I thought, how do I do that?

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:51.810
Being the face of a scientific revolution

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.130
is a lot of pressure.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:56.280
Even for Jennifer Doudna,
a legend in biology

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.650
who helped turn CRISPR
into a breakthrough method

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:02.670
for altering DNA, tackling
everything from genetic disease

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:03.810
to climate change

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:06.840
and spawning dozens of
companies in the process.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.180
But some critics claim
the tech hasn't lived up

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:10.620
to its hype just yet.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.230
Not to mention the ethical concerns.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.150
At the same time, movie depictions

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.730
of biotechnology haven't
exactly been great

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.170
PR for gene editing.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.090
More human than human is our motto.

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:22.770
It didn't matter how much I lied,

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:25.200
my real resume was in my cells.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.870
So to get to the real
story, I went to Berkeley,

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:30.930
California to meet with
Professor Doudna to talk about

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:32.850
where CRISPR is actually headed.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.280
I do have a sense that
over the next three years,

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.830
we're going to see some
continued breakthroughs

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.920
To parse through fact versus
fiction in the AI race.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:45.030
I'm not seeing chatbots in
our own experience innovating

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:48.540
And to gauge how excited
or worried we should be.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.340
So do you feel a sense of urgency?

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:52.110
Oh, yeah. Every day. Yeah.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.960
So this is your famous garden?

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:02.760
Well, such as it is, yeah.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.920
How much time do you spend back here?

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:06.570
Not enough is the answer.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:09.030
But I love being in my garden, Emily,

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.490
because it's a way I refresh my mind.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:13.680
It's amazing how when I come up here

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:17.370
and I'm, even if I'm just
pulling weeds, I feel connected

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.770
to nature in a way that
I find very relaxing.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.330
Doudna's Nobel origin story began
in the rainy town of Hilo,

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:27.210
Hawaii where her family
moved when she was seven.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:30.840
What inspired you there
when you were growing up?

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.280
Well, let's just start with, nobody in my

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:34.110
family was a scientist.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:36.180
I haven't found a single
scientist on either side

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.540
of my family, but I remember
how fascinated I was

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.460
with just the beauty of the place,

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:42.900
and I think that was one of the things

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.630
that first got me interested in science,

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:48.600
was thinking about why,
you know, different plants

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.990
and animals were able to
survive there and thrive there.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:53.190
When she's not untangling

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:55.860
life's biggest questions in her backyard,

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:57.660
Doudna is teaching, running a lab,

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:59.170
mentoring young scientists

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:00.550
and advising startups looking

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:03.940
to turn her life's work
into real world cures.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:07.720
Today, she took us inside the
Innovative Genomics Institute,

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.160
a research hub she founded

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:12.310
that's part breakthrough
lab, part startup incubator.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:13.690
It's fun, right? Yeah.

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:16.030
I still kind of get chills
when I come in the lab. Yeah.

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:17.470
You know, I, I love the feel of it.

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:20.380
I mean, I still remember my
first time going into a research

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:23.380
lab and feeling this sense
of wonder, you know, kind

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:25.780
of the sense of discoveries
are being made here

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:26.950
and wanting to be part of it.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:29.650
I can't believe I never realized this,

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:32.380
but it was pointed out
to me that your name

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:36.700
literally spells do you DNA.
I mean, that has to be fate.

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:37.700
It has
to

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:38.533
be fate, right?

00:03:38.533 --> 00:03:39.280
I had to work on this.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.080
So what kind of research
is happening right now?

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:42.640
Well, we've got all
kinds of work going on.

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.370
We're doing some very
fundamental science trying

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:49.090
to figure out the
original ancestral origins

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:52.780
of Crispr in bacteria, which
is where it comes from.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:54.650
I'm really excited about
all the students and

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:56.680
and postdoctoral trainees that are here

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:59.110
that are doing really
creative, interesting science.

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:02.140
I can't tell you how many
times they come up with ideas

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:03.370
that I would've never thought of.

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:06.160
So recently, a person
in my lab figured out

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:08.830
that he could extend the efficiency

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:10.480
of Crispr in a whole organism.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:13.810
He's doing experiments in
a mouse model of disease

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:18.100
by simply accelerating the
pace at which Crispr molecules

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:19.840
are able to edit cells.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:22.510
Wait, let's pause here for a moment.

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:24.700
Crispr, we've been talking about it,

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:26.650
but we should probably explain exactly

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:27.880
what it is and how it works.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:29.350
Right. Okay.

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:31.060
The discovery began with Doudna

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:33.760
and her collaborator,
Emmanuelle Charpentier

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:34.930
and their obsession

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:39.340
with the remarkably adaptive
immune system inside bacteria.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:41.170
When a virus attacks a bacterium,

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:44.740
the defending cell saves a
fragment of the viral DNA

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:46.690
and turns it into guide RNA,

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:49.570
which is like a tiny genetic GPS.

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:53.110
If the virus returns a
protein called CAS-9, kind

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:55.090
of like molecular scissors pairs

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:57.400
with the RNA GPS then locates

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:59.350
and cuts the invading virus.

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:00.850
The breakthrough from Doudna

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:04.390
and Charpentier was realizing
this guide, RNA GPS,

00:05:04.390 --> 00:05:07.330
could be reprogrammed
directing CAS-9 scissors

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:10.360
to cut genomic sequences beyond viral DNA,

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:12.130
including human cells.

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:15.730
Suddenly editing DNA was
no longer theoretical.

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:17.770
And over time, Doudna launched a bevy

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:19.120
of biotech companies out

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:21.910
of IGI, all working in different ways

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:25.900
to translate Crispr technology
into treatments for patients.

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:28.900
Give us a sense of the scope
of, you know, the number

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:32.260
of companies and new ventures
that have come out of IGI.

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:34.510
So, from the very beginning
of the institute, one

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:36.010
of our goals was to ensure

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:37.720
that we had a pretty smooth pipeline

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:39.820
between fundamental discoveries

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:44.080
and the kind of organizations
like startup companies

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:46.390
that would be able to
build real world value

00:05:46.390 --> 00:05:47.650
around those discoveries.

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:50.680
I think we've now spun out
31 companies at last count,

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:52.540
$9 billion valuation.

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:56.680
If you add it all up, over
2,500 jobs created largely here

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:58.700
in California, but elsewhere too.

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:00.470
It's, it's really extraordinary.

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:06.470
Long before founding IGI,
Doudna spent years studying RNA

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:07.940
at Harvard and Yale,

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:10.940
but it wasn't until 2006 at UC Berkeley

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:13.220
that she turned her attention to Crispr.

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:15.260
Were there nights that you couldn't sleep

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:16.970
because you felt you
were close to something?

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:20.240
Oh, yeah. It had that kind of
detective story feel to it.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:22.430
You know, when you're hot
on the trail of something,

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:24.590
you know that it's very
exciting, can't wait

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:26.330
to do the next experiment.

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:28.010
Crispr was very much that way.

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:31.520
What are the current use cases
that inspire you the most?

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:33.950
Well, I always think about Victoria Gray,

00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:36.920
who was the first patient
with sickle cell disease

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:39.710
to be treated with Crispr
here in the United States.

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:41.450
Her story is so inspiring.

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:43.010
Victoria Gray, an activist

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:45.710
and lecturer is the first
person with sickle cell disease

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:48.620
to receive an experimental
treatment using Crispr.

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:50.150
I want to reach others

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:54.110
who feel like no help
is coming. We're coming.

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:56.270
Do you have patients reaching
out to you all the time?

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:58.400
All the time.
So you're getting a lot of calls?

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:02.180
We do, yeah. I'll just say
that I, I always thank people

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:04.370
for reaching out because
they're often sharing very

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:05.450
personal stories.

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:06.710
They're taught, a lot of them are about

00:07:06.710 --> 00:07:08.480
children, you know, their kids.

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:10.940
A lot of people will send
pictures of their children.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:14.330
It really brings home the
importance of what we're doing,

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:16.430
and frankly, the need to work as fast

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:19.460
as possible on real therapies
that can benefit people.

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:21.260
So do you feel a sense of urgency?

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:23.330
Oh, yeah. Every day? Yeah. Yeah.

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:25.610
Because every day that
passes, you could be coming up

00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:26.443
with a miracle cure

00:07:26.443 --> 00:07:27.276
or

00:07:27.276 --> 00:07:28.109
saving someone's child.
Or

00:07:28.109 --> 00:07:28.942
just

00:07:28.942 --> 00:07:29.775
realizing

00:07:29.775 --> 00:07:31.310
that there's still a big divide

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:33.740
between the kind of work that
goes on in a lab like this

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:35.930
where we're making fundamental discoveries

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:38.240
and actually taking that knowledge

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:40.010
and having it benefit people.

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:42.380
In a real sense, there's a big gap there.

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:44.660
So far, Crispr therapies like those

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:47.300
for sickle cell have been
built to treat entire groups

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:49.820
of patients with the same genetic disease.

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:51.050
But in 2025,

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:53.240
a new milestone was
achieved at the Children's

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:54.590
Hospital of Philadelphia,

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:57.860
when an infant known as baby
KJ became the first patient

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:01.400
to receive a fully personalized
Crispr based gene therapy.

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:04.280
He was born on Thursday. Friday morning,

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:08.750
they had said to me, your
son is very sick. In patients

00:08:08.750 --> 00:08:10.340
who have urea cycle disorders,

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:11.990
they can't break down protein.

00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:13.850
And what happens is ammonia builds up

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:16.010
and ammonia can be toxic to the brain.

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:17.870
We didn't know that we were gonna reach

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:19.580
his first birthday.

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:21.620
Now we're seeing him crawl.

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:24.530
Now he's walking now he climbs
on everything under the sun

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:27.920
and thinks he's the most
funny person in the room.

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:31.070
Baby KJ's treatment shows a
glimpse of what's possible.

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:35.000
But the therapy came with a
price tag of around $800,000,

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:37.490
supported by a patchwork of
public research, funding,

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:40.250
academic collaboration and philanthropy.

00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:42.350
How's baby KJ doing?

00:08:42.350 --> 00:08:44.510
My understanding is that he's doing great.

00:08:44.510 --> 00:08:46.490
You know, he's continuing to grow.

00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:49.225
He's continuing to benefit from the,

00:08:49.225 --> 00:08:51.560
the Crispr treatment that he received.

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:52.940
It's an extraordinary story.

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:55.140
So how do we save more baby KJs?

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:56.140
Right?

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:56.973
That's the question, right?

00:08:56.973 --> 00:09:00.150
It can't be a path that
costs millions of dollars.

00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:03.120
It can't be a path that
takes a huge army of,

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:04.830
of people and time to achieve.

00:09:04.830 --> 00:09:06.210
I'd really like to see a world

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:09.450
where we can diagnose
people quite quickly,

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:11.160
who have rare disease,

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:14.040
quickly figure out if they
might be a good candidate

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:16.410
for a genetic therapy like Crispr,

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:21.210
and if the answer is yes, then
have a very smooth pipeline

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:24.720
for making the CRISPR
therapy, testing it quickly,

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:27.270
and then getting it into the patient.

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:29.430
One way Crispr research may accelerate is

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:31.110
with artificial intelligence,

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:34.230
which could theoretically
help scientists design edits

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:37.320
faster and predict their
risks more accurately.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:38.880
That promise has already piqued the

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:40.590
interest of Silicon Valley.

00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:42.450
A huge fraction of chatGPT

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:43.890
queries are health related.

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:45.570
So we wanted to get really good at this.

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:47.550
One day, maybe we can cure all

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:49.500
disease with the help of ai.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:51.150
It'll look like there's a human professor

00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:52.710
and a thousand AI grad students,

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:54.960
and they're smarter than
you are. By the way.

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:57.180
Doudna is less gung-ho on the matter.

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:58.470
Biology's hard, that's all.

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:00.660
I guess it really comes down to that.

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:03.810
You know, I've been working in,
in this area for a long time

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:05.070
as a biologist,

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:08.190
and the more I learn, honestly,
the more I'm really humbled

00:10:08.190 --> 00:10:11.130
by the fact that, you
know, biology is complex.

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:13.530
We're not going to be
able to simulate our way

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:15.510
to an understanding of the human body.

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:18.210
We're not going to be
able to avoid the need

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:19.920
for certain types of testing.

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:21.510
I do think there are opportunities

00:10:21.510 --> 00:10:25.440
to increase the efficiency
in which we make discoveries

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:28.200
about the way our bodies work

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:30.900
and the way they interact with
drugs that will be effective,

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:32.790
and I think AI will be helpful there.

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:35.490
But it's gonna come down to
training models on the right

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:38.430
kinds of data, and a big need for better

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:42.300
and more data for training
models if we wanna achieve that.

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:44.640
An OpenAI executive recently suggested

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:47.010
that if a discovery happens on chatGPT,

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:48.660
let's say a drug discovery,

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:51.270
that OpenAI should get a cut of sales.

00:10:51.270 --> 00:10:52.103
What do you think of that?

00:10:52.936 --> 00:10:53.769
Good luck.

00:10:53.769 --> 00:10:55.080
Expand.

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:57.540
How are chatbots going
to change drug discovery?

00:10:57.540 --> 00:10:59.550
I'm not sure the answer
to that yet. I don't know.

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:02.130
Lots of people are of
course, very, very hopeful,

00:11:02.130 --> 00:11:03.540
some very hopeful about it.

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:08.190
But I think that innovation
is still really in the domain

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:09.630
of human beings right now.

00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:13.830
I'm not seeing chatbots in our
own experience, innovating.

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:15.990
They can be helpful with summarizing data.

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:18.000
They can be helpful in writing reports

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:19.320
and things of that nature,

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:22.920
but I'm not seeing chatbots
coming up with a brand new idea

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:24.990
for something that nobody
else ever thought of.

00:11:24.990 --> 00:11:27.780
So you're saying AI can't innovate?

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:29.040
I don't know if it can't innovate.

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:30.660
I just don't think it is right now.

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:32.730
What about after the AGI moment?

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:34.320
Well, I never say never.

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:36.720
So maybe that'll happen, but
I'm not holding my breath.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:39.870
Well, there are just so
many big promises out there,

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:41.520
like Larry Ellison saying,

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:44.250
AI will cure cancer in a 48 hour window.

00:11:44.250 --> 00:11:45.570
Does that hold any water?

00:11:45.570 --> 00:11:48.120
Believe me, I'd be
overjoyed if that is true.

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:50.460
We all would, but I just,
I don't see it right now.

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:54.220
Doudna's mission is to turn
Crispr from a scientific

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:56.620
breakthrough into real world medicine,

00:11:56.620 --> 00:11:59.200
but commercializing gene
editing means delivering those

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:02.710
edits to the right cells
safely, reliably, and at scale.

00:12:02.710 --> 00:12:04.900
So Crispr was supposed
to usher in this era

00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:07.210
of miracle cures, and we've seen some,

00:12:07.210 --> 00:12:10.780
but was the promise of
overstated, or are we just early?

00:12:10.780 --> 00:12:13.600
I think it's a classic
example of, you know,

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:15.820
this happens a lot in in human nature.

00:12:15.820 --> 00:12:18.490
You know, we get very excited
about something, a discovery

00:12:18.490 --> 00:12:20.800
or a breakthrough of some kind,

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:24.010
and you start imagining all
the possibilities with it.

00:12:24.010 --> 00:12:27.310
And then as you start to to
dig in, you realize that,

00:12:27.310 --> 00:12:28.750
you know, there's still some challenges.

00:12:30.010 --> 00:12:32.680
I mean, think about the human
genome being sequenced back

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:34.630
around the year 2000.

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:38.710
Now we're in 2026, so we're
26 years from that moment,

00:12:38.710 --> 00:12:42.520
and yet honestly, we still
don't understand the function

00:12:42.520 --> 00:12:47.050
of about 40% of the genes
in a typical bacterial cell

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:50.110
with a much smaller genome
than the human genome.

00:12:50.110 --> 00:12:51.850
That just gives you a
little bit of a sense

00:12:51.850 --> 00:12:53.140
of the complexity.

00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:56.050
We recognize that it's a
very powerful technology,

00:12:56.050 --> 00:12:59.830
but it has limitations that
have become clear over time.

00:12:59.830 --> 00:13:02.230
What are the limitations that still exist?

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:04.240
A lot of it has to do with the way

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:07.000
that we put Crispr
molecules into patients.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:10.660
Right now, it's being done
mostly by therapies that,

00:13:10.660 --> 00:13:14.230
or strategies that involve
taking cells out of the patient,

00:13:14.230 --> 00:13:15.490
editing them in the lab,

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:17.080
and then transplanting them back in.

00:13:17.080 --> 00:13:19.420
And you can imagine that's quite involved.

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:21.790
It's quite expensive. It's
unpleasant for patients,

00:13:21.790 --> 00:13:23.170
you know, it's not ideal.

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:27.070
So I think going forward,
increasingly there are efforts

00:13:27.070 --> 00:13:30.970
to turn Crispr into what
we call an in-vivo therapy

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:33.850
or a therapeutic, where we can
deliver it directly into the

00:13:33.850 --> 00:13:35.800
patient's body in a way

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:39.160
that will take these editing
molecules to the right cells

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:42.670
where they do their job and
then don't do anything else.

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:46.120
Is there a time horizon that
you like to look at in terms

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:49.630
of when, I don't know, there,
there will be some sort

00:13:49.630 --> 00:13:53.890
of feeling that the
sky has been the limit.

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:57.280
It's always hard to put
a timeline on things.

00:13:57.280 --> 00:13:59.770
I do have a sense that
over the next three years,

00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:02.260
we're going to see some
continued breakthroughs.

00:14:02.260 --> 00:14:04.330
Two, three years isn't,
isn't too far away.

00:14:04.330 --> 00:14:06.285
Not too far away.
Yeah. So what happens in two, three

00:14:06.285 --> 00:14:07.285
years?

00:14:07.285 --> 00:14:08.118
Yeah, for one thing,
I hope that we're able

00:14:08.118 --> 00:14:10.480
to treat more patients like KJ

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:12.880
and provide a path for others.

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:16.030
We'd like to get more medical
centers involved in this

00:14:16.030 --> 00:14:17.410
type of an approach.

00:14:17.410 --> 00:14:20.380
We'd like to drive down the
cost, of course, partnering

00:14:20.380 --> 00:14:21.520
with manufacturers

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:25.090
that can make the molecules
we need more inexpensively,

00:14:25.090 --> 00:14:28.600
and really just getting
more clinical teams

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:30.250
involved in this effort.

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:32.770
I think if that can be done,
then there's really going

00:14:32.770 --> 00:14:34.390
to be a path to opening the door

00:14:34.390 --> 00:14:36.460
for many more rare diseases to be treated.

00:14:37.750 --> 00:14:41.140
But these large scale efforts
require hefty resources.

00:14:41.140 --> 00:14:42.190
And over the past year,

00:14:42.190 --> 00:14:44.590
federal funding cuts under
the Trump administration have

00:14:44.590 --> 00:14:46.450
created significant uncertainty

00:14:46.450 --> 00:14:48.190
for long-term scientific research.

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:50.680
Over the past three years,

00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:54.740
the number new grants
from the NSF dropped 24%,

00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:57.650
and roughly 25,000 scientists and staff.

00:14:57.650 --> 00:15:00.140
About 20% of the workforce
have left federal

00:15:00.140 --> 00:15:01.850
research agencies.

00:15:01.850 --> 00:15:04.700
We're a year into the
Trump administration.

00:15:04.700 --> 00:15:07.370
A number of grants have been cut.

00:15:07.370 --> 00:15:10.430
How does science fair in
the age of President Trump?

00:15:10.430 --> 00:15:11.810
We're seeing real,

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:13.850
real challenges right now,
as you just mentioned.

00:15:13.850 --> 00:15:15.080
You know, I think the, the cuts

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:18.590
that we've seen really
risk the economic success

00:15:18.590 --> 00:15:20.510
of the United States in, in science

00:15:20.510 --> 00:15:23.870
and technology in a way that
is, to me, very unfortunate.

00:15:23.870 --> 00:15:26.810
We've been a leader in science
since the second World War.

00:15:26.810 --> 00:15:30.405
On every dollar that's been
invested from the NIH in

00:15:30.405 --> 00:15:34.940
research leads to about $2.50
of economic benefit.

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:38.090
That's a pretty darn good
return on investment.

00:15:38.090 --> 00:15:41.870
So giving that up, I think
seeding that to other countries

00:15:41.870 --> 00:15:44.120
because they will, they will
step in, you know, there,

00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:45.320
there are opportunities there.

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:47.510
If we don't continue our investment in

00:15:47.510 --> 00:15:49.460
science, others will do so.

00:15:49.460 --> 00:15:51.500
I think that would really be a shame.

00:15:52.460 --> 00:15:55.250
But the Trump administration's
impact on science goes way

00:15:55.250 --> 00:15:56.660
beyond these cuts.

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:58.730
What do you think of the MAHA movement?

00:15:58.730 --> 00:16:01.850
Is it gonna really make
Americans healthier

00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:03.680
or something else?

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:05.750
Look, I think, you know,
health decisions need

00:16:05.750 --> 00:16:08.450
to be made based on data
and based on science.

00:16:08.450 --> 00:16:11.390
And there's a reason why
people train in medical schools

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:14.240
or they go to graduate
school to learn about how

00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:17.630
to evaluate science or
to evaluate clinical data

00:16:17.630 --> 00:16:19.280
or to work with patients.

00:16:19.280 --> 00:16:20.990
And I think we have to lean into

00:16:20.990 --> 00:16:22.580
that when we make health decisions.

00:16:22.580 --> 00:16:24.860
Do you know RFK JR?
I don't.

00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:27.500
If you had an audience with
him, what would you say?

00:16:27.500 --> 00:16:28.820
I'd say what I just said to you,

00:16:28.820 --> 00:16:31.130
What are your concerns about
where this leads, you know,

00:16:31.130 --> 00:16:34.040
there's the MAHA movement,
there's the anti-vax movement

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:36.650
coming from somebody who
worked on RNA, which was a key

00:16:36.650 --> 00:16:38.240
to the COVID vaccine.

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:40.520
It's a dangerous moment.
It's very dangerous.

00:16:40.520 --> 00:16:42.860
I mean, when I see things
like measles outbreak in

00:16:42.860 --> 00:16:44.810
South Carolina, it's terrible.

00:16:44.810 --> 00:16:46.790
It doesn't have to be that way.

00:16:46.790 --> 00:16:49.340
When you're in a room alone
with scientists, you know,

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:51.140
what do you privately discuss?

00:16:51.140 --> 00:16:53.930
Like what are people
saying about this moment?

00:16:53.930 --> 00:16:55.220
Well, I think there's a lot of concern.

00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:59.660
I think that many of us feel,
if I'm honest, also a sense

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:01.280
of responsibility that we need

00:17:01.280 --> 00:17:04.280
to be better at communicating
the value of our work.

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:05.870
Why it's publicly funded.

00:17:05.870 --> 00:17:08.450
Why should taxpayer dollars
be used for research?

00:17:08.450 --> 00:17:10.730
What's the value of what we do?

00:17:10.730 --> 00:17:13.040
And there's incredible value,

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:14.960
but we haven't maybe been very good

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:17.720
as a community at
communicating that to people

00:17:17.720 --> 00:17:19.490
that are not scientists.

00:17:19.490 --> 00:17:20.750
Does it ever make you wanna scream?

00:17:21.740 --> 00:17:24.920
Yes, but you know, then
Emily, I look around

00:17:24.920 --> 00:17:27.980
and I think, you know, I
work at an incredible place

00:17:27.980 --> 00:17:30.050
and I feel also incredibly grateful.

00:17:33.650 --> 00:17:35.420
We talked a bit about biotech funding.

00:17:35.420 --> 00:17:37.280
Researchers say they're
considering leaving

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:38.930
the United States.

00:17:38.930 --> 00:17:42.530
What are the implications of
this? Does China surge ahead?

00:17:42.530 --> 00:17:45.020
I think it maybe does, at least initially.

00:17:45.020 --> 00:17:46.310
I think that's a possibility.

00:17:46.310 --> 00:17:48.440
I think it's a very real possibility

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:49.470
because at the same time

00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:51.840
that the United States is
pulling back on funding,

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:53.245
we're seeing a, you know,

00:17:53.245 --> 00:17:56.100
a big investment in science in China.

00:17:56.100 --> 00:17:57.150
Over the past decade,

00:17:57.150 --> 00:18:00.450
China's biotech industry has
rapidly accelerated, propelled

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:01.860
by state backed investment

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:05.490
and a streamlined path
for human trials in 2018,

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:06.690
amid this climb,

00:18:06.690 --> 00:18:10.050
a scientist based in Shenzhen
conducted a widely condemned

00:18:10.050 --> 00:18:11.310
gene editing experiment.

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:14.130
The Chinese researcher has
created an international

00:18:14.130 --> 00:18:17.070
controversy after claiming he
helped make the world's first

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:18.930
genetically edited babies.

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:22.350
The procedure raised fears of
a new era of designer babies

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:25.140
with parents selecting
traits for their children,

00:18:25.140 --> 00:18:28.500
A possibility that remains
largely theoretical,

00:18:28.500 --> 00:18:30.390
although maybe not for long.

00:18:30.390 --> 00:18:34.650
You've said, we'll see Crispr
edited babies within 25 years.

00:18:34.650 --> 00:18:38.160
What kind of edits and
who are these babies?

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:40.440
Those decisions are
gonna be made by a, a lot

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:42.960
of the people who are coming of age and,

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:45.210
and coming into re their
reproductive years.

00:18:45.210 --> 00:18:48.930
I think because it'll be a
question of assuming that we get

00:18:48.930 --> 00:18:51.780
to a point where the
technology is actually safe

00:18:51.780 --> 00:18:55.620
and effective for that kind
of purpose, then asking

00:18:55.620 --> 00:18:57.270
what kinds of edits should we make?

00:18:57.270 --> 00:18:59.970
And who should have
access to that technology?

00:18:59.970 --> 00:19:02.370
Who should be paying for that technology?

00:19:02.370 --> 00:19:04.260
Who should be regulating that technology?

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:06.900
Those questions really haven't
been answered at this stage.

00:19:06.900 --> 00:19:08.880
I know people of reproductive age now

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:11.280
and thinking about some of
these things, the companies

00:19:11.280 --> 00:19:13.740
that are promising they can select embryos

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:16.110
for traits like intelligence
and other things.

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:19.050
It's part of what's been
called the Silicon Valley push

00:19:19.050 --> 00:19:20.640
to breed super babies.

00:19:20.640 --> 00:19:22.560
We look at something like
height, even eye color,

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:24.780
hair color, intelligence, intelligence.

00:19:24.780 --> 00:19:28.320
What's actually possible
now and what's not possible

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:30.030
because aren't these
traits that are controlled

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:31.830
by thousands of genes?

00:19:31.830 --> 00:19:34.470
Yes. So I agree.

00:19:34.470 --> 00:19:37.170
I think it's, I think it's,
it's a tall order to be able

00:19:37.170 --> 00:19:39.720
to do that kind of
selection in an in vitro

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:41.280
fertilization clinic.

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:43.080
It's also, you know,

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:46.230
likewise going to be very
hard to know which sets

00:19:46.230 --> 00:19:50.160
of genes would need to be
edited to give you a, an outcome

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:53.940
that affected intelligence, or
something as complex as that.

00:19:53.940 --> 00:19:56.760
So I just don't see it anytime soon.

00:19:56.760 --> 00:19:58.980
There's this concern if
you select for one trait,

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:02.490
let's say intelligence,
that you might also end up

00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:04.620
getting a lot of undesirable traits.

00:20:04.620 --> 00:20:06.060
Well, you know that you're touching on

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:07.350
something that I think about a lot.

00:20:07.350 --> 00:20:10.740
You know, all the genes in
our cells are interacting

00:20:10.740 --> 00:20:13.920
with each other in ways that
we mostly aren't aware of

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:15.330
and don't understand yet.

00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:18.180
And that means that if we tweak one gene

00:20:18.180 --> 00:20:21.390
or even a few, we can't always be sure

00:20:21.390 --> 00:20:22.950
what the outcome is gonna be,

00:20:22.950 --> 00:20:25.470
especially if we're looking
long into the future.

00:20:25.470 --> 00:20:28.710
So I think that's gonna continue
to be an area where we need

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:30.120
to do research.

00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:33.330
You know, clearly that work is
still at a very early stage.

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:35.640
They're working in mice and
you know, they're working in,

00:20:35.640 --> 00:20:37.410
in systems that, you
know, they haven't gotten

00:20:37.410 --> 00:20:39.180
to the complexity of a human body yet.

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:42.690
So let's say it becomes
increasingly possible.

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:45.090
On what grounds can we tell
parents, you can't do this?

00:20:45.090 --> 00:20:48.760
Like, why wouldn't you want
to engineer babies to be

00:20:48.760 --> 00:20:50.200
as healthy as possible?

00:20:50.200 --> 00:20:51.790
Some people might say, well, I'd like

00:20:51.790 --> 00:20:55.270
to edit out the sickle cell
trait, which, you know,

00:20:55.270 --> 00:20:57.220
I think one could make
a very good argument

00:20:57.220 --> 00:20:58.720
for wanting to do that.

00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:02.110
On the other hand, if a parent
said, I want to, you know,

00:21:02.110 --> 00:21:05.800
change something about eye color or height

00:21:05.800 --> 00:21:09.040
or musculature, you know, that, you know,

00:21:09.040 --> 00:21:12.430
some people might feel that
it's not appropriate in the end.

00:21:12.430 --> 00:21:17.410
I think it's gonna come
down to probably putting

00:21:17.410 --> 00:21:20.770
applications of Crispr
into different buckets.

00:21:20.770 --> 00:21:24.100
You know, where we could
imagine one bucket would be

00:21:24.100 --> 00:21:26.590
for diseases that are devastating,

00:21:26.590 --> 00:21:29.530
where we have a very
well-defined genetic cause

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:33.970
and a clear way to make the
targeted change in the genome

00:21:33.970 --> 00:21:37.570
that would prevent that with
Crispr versus other things

00:21:37.570 --> 00:21:39.610
that have a lot of risk
associated with them,

00:21:39.610 --> 00:21:43.420
where you might need to be
changing many sets of genes that,

00:21:43.420 --> 00:21:45.400
as you said, would potentially lead

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:46.840
to unintended consequences.

00:21:49.330 --> 00:21:52.750
You've been called the
moral compass of biotech.

00:21:53.830 --> 00:21:57.550
Does it ever feel more like a
burden and less like an honor?

00:21:57.550 --> 00:22:00.730
And do you ever miss the sort
of quiet joy of discovery?

00:22:00.730 --> 00:22:02.680
Yes, I miss the quiet joy of discovery.

00:22:02.680 --> 00:22:05.440
I'd be lying
if I didn't admit that,

00:22:05.440 --> 00:22:07.540
but these are kind of the
cards I've been dealt in my

00:22:07.540 --> 00:22:10.000
career, and so I try to embrace it.

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:11.890
I try to learn from it.

00:22:11.890 --> 00:22:14.680
I try to use it as a way to, you know,

00:22:14.680 --> 00:22:17.080
to ultimately make sure
that CRISPR is gonna benefit

00:22:17.080 --> 00:22:18.310
as many people as possible.

00:22:19.420 --> 00:22:22.150
What began with innovation
in a lab has grown into an

00:22:22.150 --> 00:22:26.140
entire Crispr industry,
startups, pharmaceutical giants,

00:22:26.140 --> 00:22:27.940
and now AI driven companies,

00:22:27.940 --> 00:22:31.390
all converging on the next
phase in gene editing.

00:22:31.390 --> 00:22:33.640
But for many, the biggest concern isn't

00:22:33.640 --> 00:22:35.530
failure, it's success.

00:22:36.370 --> 00:22:39.010
Do you ever struggle with
unleashing this power on the

00:22:39.010 --> 00:22:40.900
world, this potential to do so much good,

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:43.510
but also it's really scary.

00:22:43.510 --> 00:22:45.580
I guess I don't tend to
think about it that way.

00:22:45.580 --> 00:22:48.400
I, I tend to think about it
in terms of the opportunities

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:49.450
that Crispr brings,

00:22:49.450 --> 00:22:52.360
but I also recognize that
there's a responsibility

00:22:52.360 --> 00:22:55.510
to speak out about the
potential risk at the moment.

00:22:55.510 --> 00:22:59.350
Certainly I feel very excited
about where it's headed.

